NAME
       podman-systemd.unit - Quadlet files: containers run by systemd

SYNOPSIS
       ~/.config/containers/systemd/NAME.container   (a rootless user)
       /etc/containers/systemd/NAME.container        (root)

DESCRIPTION
       Quadlet lets you describe a container in a small file and have systemd
       run it as a service. At boot and at every systemctl daemon-reload, a
       systemd generator reads each NAME.container file and makes a service
       unit NAME.service from it. You never write the podman run command
       yourself.

       Because the service is generated, it is not enabled with systemctl
       enable: the [Install] section of the .container file says which
       target starts it. For a rootless user that is default.target, and the
       user needs lingering (loginctl enable-linger USER) for it to start at
       boot without a login. For root use multi-user.target.

       The file uses the same layout as other unit files: [Unit] and
       [Service] work as usual, and the container itself is described in the
       [Container] section.

[CONTAINER] KEYS
       Image=IMAGE
              The image to run (required). Give the full name with the
              registry.

       ContainerName=NAME
              The container's name. The default is systemd-NAME.

       PublishPort=HOSTPORT:CONTAINERPORT
              Publish a port, like podman run -p (repeatable).

       Volume=HOSTDIR:CONTAINERDIR[:OPTIONS]
              Mount a directory or named volume, like podman run -v; add :Z
              for the SELinux label (repeatable).

       Environment=NAME=VALUE
              Set an environment variable (repeatable).

       Exec=COMMAND
              The command to run instead of the image's default.

OTHER SECTIONS
       [Unit] Description=TEXT
              The service's description.

       [Service] Restart=always|on-failure|no
              Restart the container when it stops.

       [Install] WantedBy=default.target
              Start the service with this target.

EXAMPLES
       As student (logged in through ssh student@localhost), run the httpd
       image as a service that starts at boot:

           mkdir -p ~/.config/containers/systemd ~/web
           vi ~/.config/containers/systemd/web.container

       with this content:

           [Unit]
           Description=Web server in a container

           [Container]
           Image=registry.lab.example.com/ubi9/httpd-24
           ContainerName=web
           PublishPort=8080:8080
           Volume=/home/student/web:/var/www/html:Z

           [Service]
           Restart=always

           [Install]
           WantedBy=default.target

       then:

           systemctl --user daemon-reload
           systemctl --user start web.service
           systemctl --user status web.service
           loginctl enable-linger student

       Show the unit that Quadlet generated:

           systemctl --user cat web.service

FILES
       ~/.config/containers/systemd/
              A rootless user's Quadlet files.

       /etc/containers/systemd/
              Root's Quadlet files.

SEE ALSO
       podman(1), podman-run(1), podman-generate-systemd(1), systemctl(1),
       systemd.unit(5), loginctl(1)
